"comparison":"Both companies address infrastructure demands driven by AI data centers, but focus on different layers of the stack. Micron emphasizes 'DDR5' offering 'improved bandwidth and power efficiency' for 'data-intensive applications' (mu_2024_1_P007_S002), which relates to the data storage/access bottleneck. Broadcom explicitly addresses the 'connectivity bottlenecks in data centers, particularly in AI data centers where compute bandwidth and cluster sizes grow rapidly' through its 'Ethernet network interface card (NIC) and switching solution' (avgo_2024_1_P008_S003). While both cite AI-driven bandwidth demands, Micron's disclosure focuses on memory bandwidth/efficiency and Broadcom on network connectivity/switching.",
